<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis Peirson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1757–1781</span> · Also known as Major Francis Peirson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis Peirson was a British army officer who commanded the troops in Jersey when a French force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-de-rullecourt">Philippe de Rullecourt</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helier">Saint Helier</a> on 6 January 1781. Disregarding the surrender signed by the lieutenant-governor, the 24-year-old major led regulars and militia against the invaders and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jersey">Battle of Jersey</a>, but was killed at the moment of victory. Copley's painting The Death of Major Peirson commemorates him.</p></div>
